Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.43,0:00:05.11,Default,,0,0,0,,Let's explore a small demonstration We encode logical\Ninformation into three physical
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.12,0:00:09.88,Default,,0,0,0,,qubits and deliberately introduce one bit-flip error A\Nrecovery circuit can then return
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.89,0:00:14.75,Default,,0,0,0,,the logical qubit to its prepared state It is important\Nnot to exaggerate this conclusion
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.45,0:00:19.42,Default,,0,0,0,,This simple repetition code corrects one X-type error\Nunder the conditions of the example
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.11,0:00:24.16,Default,,0,0,0,,It does not automatically correct every error does not\Nprotect against arbitrary noise
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.36,0:00:28.44,Default,,0,0,0,,and does not demonstrate a fault-tolerant computer We\Nare also not making three
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.45,0:00:33.01,Default,,0,0,0,,independent copies of an unknown quantum state We are\Nencoding the information in a joint
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.02,0:00:34.77,Default,,0,0,0,,state respecting quantum rules